scenarios.modelAnswer:
Separate the two questions the Profiler answers: did it render, and did it have to. On the keystroke, `UserList` had to render — `filter` is one of its inputs and its output changes. `UserProfile` did not: it rendered because its parent rendered, and the commit detail says its inputs are identical, so that is an avoidable render. On the row click it is the reverse: `UserProfile` re-renders with a new `selected`, which is legitimate, and `UserList` re-renders with the same inputs, which is not. `Toolbar` and `Sidebar` are cheap per render, but they are why the waste travels: every value is threaded through them, so any change above walks the whole spine, and the structural symptom (a new prop in three signatures, a new level touching three components) is the same defect seen from the code side.

Fixed cheaply, with composition and colocation: the owner of the state passes elements, not data, and the search state moves to the smallest component that contains both the input and the list.

```tsx
function UserWorkspace({ users }: { users: User[] }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<User | null>(null);
  return (
    <Shell
      sidebar={<UserPanel users={users} onSelect={setSelected} />}
      detail={<UserProfile user={selected} />}
    />
  );
}

function Shell({ sidebar, detail }: { sidebar: ReactNode; detail: ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <div className="shell">
      <header>…</header>
      <aside>{sidebar}</aside>
      <main>{detail}</main>
    </div>
  );
}

function UserPanel({ users, onSelect }: UserPanelProps) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');   // the input and the list share one owner
  return (
    <>
      <SearchInput value={filter} onChange={setFilter} />
      <UserList users={users} filter={filter} onSelect={onSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

What that buys: `Shell` never appears in a commit caused by `selected` or `filter`; adding a layout level edits `Shell` only; `selected` appears in one signature, not four; and a keystroke now renders `UserPanel`'s subtree, leaving `UserProfile` out of that render path entirely. The honest limit is that composition alone does not stop a sibling render when a shared owner re-renders — it removes the plumbing and lets you shrink the render path. If product requires the search to stay in the global header while the list stays in the sidebar, the two consumers cannot be co-located, and that is the case for the next step.

When consumers sit far apart (header search, list, profile panel, a badge in a table, a breadcrumb), put one owner around the subtree and let them subscribe:

```tsx
const SelectedUserContext = createContext<{ selected: User | null } | null>(null);
const UserActionsContext = createContext<((u: User | null) => void) | null>(null);

function useSelectedUser() {
  const ctx = useContext(SelectedUserContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('useSelectedUser must be used inside <UserProvider>');
  return ctx;
}

function UserProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<User | null>(null);
  // state changes, the setter does not: two contexts, so dispatch-only rows never re-render
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ selected }), [selected]);
  return (
    <UserActionsContext value={setSelected}>
      <SelectedUserContext value={value}>{children}</SelectedUserContext>
    </UserActionsContext>
  );
}
```

Each piece has a job: the provider is the single owner; `useMemo` keeps the value's identity stable across commits where `selected` did not change; splitting state from actions means a row button that only dispatches stops subscribing to the changing value; and the guard hook fails loudly instead of reading a silent `null` default. If this becomes a reusable widget, the compound shape (`<UserPicker>`, `<UserPicker.List>`, `<UserPicker.Profile>`, context private to the family) makes app code compose parts and thread nothing. Be explicit about the limit: with `[selected]` as the dependency, every reader of `SelectedUserContext` still re-renders when the selection changes — that is correct, the value they read changed. Context removes plumbing, it does not make subscribers selective, so a large or fast-changing value wants a store with selectors instead.

Without `useMemo`, `value={{ selected }}` is a new object every time the provider renders. A provider also re-renders for reasons that have nothing to do with the selection (a parent update, a sibling's state), `Object.is` sees a different object, and every consumer re-renders with unchanged data. `memo` on a consumer will not save it: memo skips renders caused by the parent's props, and a context update is delivered directly to the reader. That is the "my Context re-renders everything" bug, and the symptom is visible in the Profiler as consumers rendering with identical inputs on unrelated commits. The fix order is: shrink the provider to the subtree that needs it, split by change rate (state versus actions), split by domain, and only then stabilise identity.

When Context is the wrong tool: one or two levels of props is not drilling, it is a dependency you can read at the call site; a single consumer wants a prop; navigable selection (`/users/42`) belongs in the URL, where it survives a reload and can be shared; a large value that changes often belongs in a store with selectors or a server cache, because every context reader re-renders; and a wrapper that only carries content is a `children` slot, not data — that is the first fix again. Finally, re-record the same two interactions and compare commit counts: the keystroke should touch only the search/list subtree, and a row click should only touch rows that actually display the selection.
